      "feedback": "This is a **good species to have a page for**, and the raw material is stronger than average. The Bengali sap story, the spines, and the male/female distinction give it real visitor value. But I would make several fairly substantial changes. The biggest problem is not style; it is that some of the botanical/geographic claims are more confident than the evidence warrants.\n\n## My ranking\n\n### 1. Fix the native-range statement — **high priority**\n\nThe current:\n\n> \"Native to the Indian subcontinent — primarily India and southern Pakistan...\"\n\nis defensible in some horticultural references, but it is not cleanly aligned with current Kew POWO, which currently gives the native range as **southwestern India and Sri Lanka**. Other authoritative horticultural sources give a broader Indian-subcontinent range, including southern Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, etc. ([Plants of the World Online][1])\n\nSo I would **not present the geography as settled fact** in the current detailed form.\n\nAlso, the phra",
